## Required behaviour

- Give the dialog a clear accessible title.
- Move focus into the dialog when it opens.
- Keep focus within the active modal while the background is inert.
- Provide an accessible close or cancel action.
- Support Escape where cancelling is safe.
- Return focus to the triggering control when the dialog closes.
- Keep content scrollable without hiding the heading or actions.

## Confirm consequential actions

State the specific consequence. Use “Delete automation” instead of “Are you sure?” as the main action label. Keep the safe cancellation action easy to find.

Do not interrupt routine appearance changes or ordinary light controls with unnecessary confirmations. Preserve existing HA confirmations for locks, alarms and other consequential controls.

## Visual treatment

Use the shared type, field, button, border and focus tokens. Use a subdued backdrop to separate the dialog from the page, with enough contrast for the dialog boundary. Do not strip modal positioning, focus trapping or scrolling behaviour while removing decorative elevation.

## Source status

The [MoJ modal dialog](https://design-patterns.service.justice.gov.uk/components/modal-dialog/) documents inert background and trapped focus. It is currently experimental. civica.uk borrows those interaction requirements; it does not substitute the MoJ HTML for native Home Assistant dialogs or claim that MoJ's testing covers this implementation.
